### How do you convert string numbers to integers?

Use Integer::parseInt as a static method reference in map or mapToInt.

```java
package com.bank.ops;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class ParseStringsToInts {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> amounts = List.of("100", "250", "75", "300");

        // Stream<Integer> via map
        List<Integer> boxed = amounts.stream()
            .map(Integer::parseInt)
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println("Boxed: " + boxed);

        // Primitive IntStream via mapToInt
        int total = amounts.stream()
            .mapToInt(Integer::parseInt)
            .sum();
        System.out.println("Total: " + total);
    }
}
```

```text
Boxed: [100, 250, 75, 300]
Total: 725
```

### What is the difference between map and mapToInt?

| Method | Result type | Boxing | Numeric operations |
|---|---|---|---|
| map(Integer::parseInt) | Stream<Integer> | Yes (autoboxed) | None (need collect) |
| mapToInt(Integer::parseInt) | IntStream (primitive) | No | sum(), average(), max() |

Use mapToInt when you need numeric aggregations to avoid boxing overhead.

### How do you handle malformed input?

parseInt throws NumberFormatException on non-numeric strings, aborting the entire stream.

```java
// WRONG: one bad value crashes the whole stream
List<String> dirty = List.of("100", "abc", "300");
int total = dirty.stream()
    .mapToInt(Integer::parseInt)  // throws on "abc"!
    .sum();

// CORRECT: wrap in a helper that returns Optional
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.OptionalInt;

int safeTotal = dirty.stream()
    .map(s -> {
        try {
            return OptionalInt.of(Integer.parseInt(s));
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            return OptionalInt.empty();
        }
    })
    .filter(OptionalInt::isPresent)
    .mapToInt(OptionalInt::getAsInt)
    .sum();
System.out.println("Safe total: " + safeTotal);  // 400
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using Integer::parseInt when Integer::valueOf is more appropriate.

```java
// parseInt returns primitive int
int primitive = Integer.parseInt("42");

// valueOf returns Integer object (cached for -128 to 127)
Integer boxed = Integer.valueOf("42");

// In a stream with map, both work; mapToInt requires ToIntFunction.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- A static method reference has no receiver; the argument flows directly to the static method.
- parseInt is the right choice for mapToInt (returns primitive int). valueOf is better when you want the cached Integer object.
- For production data pipelines, never use a bare parseInt in a stream without error handling. One bad record should not abort the entire batch.
- Choosing a static method reference assumes the inputs are clean or that failing fast is acceptable.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you convert to Double instead?" Use Double::parseDouble or mapToDouble.
- "How do you handle leading/trailing whitespace?" Trim first: .map(String::trim).mapToInt(Integer::parseInt).
- "What about very large numbers?" parseInt overflows for values outside int range; use Long::parseLong or BigInteger.
